WEBVTT

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:30.200
 As a program that's really concerned about inclusivity, we need to figure out how to engage all of our kids in an environment that might be chaotic, you know, the kitchen, the garden, there's a lot of distractions, there's a lot of opportunity for risk and mistakes, and within those experiences, we invite the kids to be empowered with their own choices, you know, how will you approach this problem or mistake, and how will you respond?

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.010
 And we support them in being their own their own leader in their in the world.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:43.010
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.050
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:50.420
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.570
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:01:00.220
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms,

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:04.140
 kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.860
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:13.420
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.510
 Jesra Thompson is the program supervisor of the Berkeley Public School Gardening and Cooking Program,

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:29.590
 where she leads a team of garden educators and works with schools and community organizations to provide hands-on, place-based education to all students.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:36.370
 She is a food systems planner who focuses on community development, land use planning, and education.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:46.370
 Jesra has worked on healthy food access and education at DC Greens, the California Farmers Market Association, and the USDA's Food and Nutrition Service.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:50.070
 Welcome to the show, Jesra.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.390
 Jesra, it's great to be here.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:52.830
 Thank you so much for having me.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:53.470
 I'm Rebecca.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.870
 I'm also working on the show for this season.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:57.710
 Very excited to do so.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:01.190
 And we're here with Jesra, who's actually my boss.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:07.070
 And I will start off with, can you start maybe describing your work with the Berkeley School Gardening and Cooking Program?

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.320
 Sure.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:14.560
 For the last three years, I've led the Berkeley Unified School District Gardening and Cooking Program.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:22.520
 And we have been educating all of our students from preschool through high school in 17 school gardens,

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:27.930
 and now four kitchen classrooms with Rebecca and some other folks' help.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:39.450
 And we really focus on integrating academic standards as support to students and teachers that are challenged with so many different students in our school district,

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.450
 and they have so many different needs, and they come from so many different backgrounds.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:50.670
 And we're all kind of struggling together in our larger school district community on how to approach that and how do we support each child.

00:02:50.850 --> 00:03:00.170
 And so we are collaborating with all the teachers and principals and other community supports on meeting each child where they're at and focusing on whole child development.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:09.450
 And that includes not just the standards, but also social-emotional care, nutrition, having them talk about the environment and their own place in the world.

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.690
 We're really focusing on doing that these last three years.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:13.600
 Great.

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:17.920
 And can you tell us more about what one of these classes would look like?

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:19.920
 Yeah.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:26.280
 So in our gardens, we have a curriculum that we just published two years ago,

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:33.230
 and it teaches science and language and math in the gardens from preschool through middle school.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.660
 And the kids come out to the garden for about an hour every other week,

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.780
 and they come out there with their classroom teacher,

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:49.040
 and they're instructed by a garden instructor who's got a wealth of experience and knowledge about gardening and education in these fields.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.920
 And they will possibly talk about plant parts and photosynthesis,

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.920
 and there'll be a quick kind of intro of this is what we're going to learn today so the kids know what to expect.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.720
 And they then go into different activities, whether it's like small group or team building or one-on-one,

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:12.560
 and they experiment and explore more about, say, photosynthesis or take apart a flower to learn more about plant parts.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:16.820
 And then they go back into their classroom, and hopefully they have more questions the next week.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:23.680
 Because I work with the cooking program, can you maybe go into what differentiates the gardening from the cooking program?

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.790
 Yeah, I mean, this is something that we're trying new this year,

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:32.390
 and we are bringing cooking and nutrition education into our after-school programming.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:37.830
 So we're really trying to figure out how we're doing it for the first time and after school.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.610
 And we're doing this with a grant from the Berkeley Soda Tax.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:48.750
 The grant commission is really focused on reducing sugar consumption and increasing sugar awareness.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.830
 So we're also trying to figure out how to do that the best way we possibly can.

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:55.150
 And we're putting those in four schools as opposed to all of our schools,

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.630
 because we want to do that in such a way where we could have assessments.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.450
 We could measure and track what we're doing and incorporate change if necessary.

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:09.170
 So right now, our core group of collaborators includes Rebecca as our cooking support

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:14.110
 and a health and wellness coordinator who's a registered dietitian and another cooking support

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:17.460
 and some curriculum development supports.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:26.060
 And we are using a whole gleaned base of lessons that we can pull from

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.200
 that touch on not just nutrition and sugar consumption,

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.520
 but also changing habits within the student population and their families.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:39.000
 So a cooking class or a nutrition and cooking class after school,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:43.450
 and you might be able to talk more about what the experience is like from your perspective,

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:49.790
 but when I'm out there, what I see is the kids are out in after school

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.170
 and they get brought into our kitchen classrooms.

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:58.870
 And some of the schools have more kitchen materials like a stove or a fridge

00:05:58.870 --> 00:05:59.870
 and others don't.

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.170
 So it's real, it's real, it's real life.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.510
 It's not like we have this huge amount of money and we're a foundation

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:11.550
 or we're a nonprofit that is able to do these really boutique classes in a very well-equipped space.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:15.490
 We have varying spaces, which I think is a great opportunity in our garden program

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.650
 and in our cooking program.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.950
 So we can model what this looks like across the board.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:22.010
 And so when I was there, you guys were talking about

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:25.970
 all the different nutrients you need to grow big and strong.

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:29.470
 And this was with, I think, a third grade class or first grade class.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:35.230
 You guys were scaling it down really well where it was just like so much.

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.990
 And that's what's, that's the beauty of our, of our collaboration right now is that we're like

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.250
 trying a few things out.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:42.410
 We're noticing how we're doing it.

00:06:42.450 --> 00:06:47.210
 We're scaling it up and down because when you're teaching a lesson, you know, one day to first grade

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:52.990
 and then the next day to a fifth grade, that becomes way overwhelming and the content gets lost.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:57.790
 So we tried to scale content up and down and you guys were scaling way down the,

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:00.030
 what do you need to be big and strong?

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:04.770
 You had the grow, glow, and what was this?

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:05.750
 Go.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.910
 That was also hard to teach first graders.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.330
 They couldn't remember the difference between the words.

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:16.390
 The great part about that was that it was participatory, right?

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:21.890
 So they want to support you when they see you struggling with that and you want to support

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.470
 them when they're struggling with that.

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:26.790
 And then together, everyone's like chanting, grow, glow, go.

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:35.550
 And they're knowing about, you know, protein and carbohydrates and sugars and how that's like

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.730
 the negative effect of all of those things.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:42.250
 And you do it in such a way where it's kind of like this, this kind of performance circus.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:46.630
 And then the kids get a quick demo of what they're going to do that day.

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:50.490
 And they cook a recipe that's related to the topic.

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:57.010
 So they made tacos with squash and beans and salsa.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:01.690
 And the kids are learning knife skills and we're learning how to work together.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:03.490
 And they're chopping up the different vegetables.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.230
 And the instructors are sauteing and cooking and everyone is eating together.

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:11.890
 And during the meal, people are having conversations.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:18.910
 The littles and the bigs and everyone is talking about what they just learned, which then reinforces

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.150
 in a really casual environment, what we're trying to do.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:26.300
 Jazza, you started off by talking about kind of the whole child education.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:29.280
 And I guess this is a question for both of you.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:33.500
 What's possible in a garden or in a kitchen that's not possible in a classroom?

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.060
 So much.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:42.930
 I think for me, I've been working with kids in a cooking setting for around two years now,

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:44.030
 maybe a little more.

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:48.270
 I started working as a volunteer with 18 Reasons doing Cooking Matters classes.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:52.470
 And I've just seen kids just light up in a cooking environment.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:58.190
 Like you don't tell them no that often unless it's in concerns to their safety.

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.590
 Like if they're holding a knife wrong, you'll say no.

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:05.770
 But in general, I have found that in a cooking environment, I can say yes to kids a lot.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.330
 Like, yes, you can touch that.

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:08.710
 Yes, you can eat that.

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:11.810
 Yes, you can do that as long as you wash your hands first.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:25.410
 So I think for a kid hearing that they can do so many things, it really positively, as Jazza said, positively reinforces sort of being able to cook for yourself and provide for yourself.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:36.470
 And I just see kids get so excited that they've made something or that they can decide what they want to put in their taco or that they can decide what they want to put on top of their pancake.

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:38.890
 And it's just, it's amazing.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:48.750
 I think having an environment where kids can be standing, moving around, and actively engaging with their classmates as well as the teacher as opposed to sitting in a chair listening to one person talk.

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:55.410
 Yeah, and that power of choice is really powerful and important for child development.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:02.870
 A lot of kids don't have that option of choice, or they live in an environment that feels very chaotic, whether it's at school or at home.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:12.260
 And to offer them an opportunity to make a choice and empower them with that is really meaningful and goes way beyond what we can measure in the classroom.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:18.170
 And on a macro level, we think about all the different learners in the district.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:22.710
 We are an inclusive district, meaning that we don't have special ed classes.

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:29.550
 We engage all of our learners in one classroom, and we provide them with different supports, whether teachers or instructors.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:39.920
 And as a program that's really concerned about inclusivity, we need to figure out how to engage all of our kids in an environment that might be chaotic.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.440
 You know, the kitchen, the garden, there's a lot of distractions.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:48.450
 There's a lot of opportunity for risk and mistakes.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:53.590
 And within those experiences, we invite the kids to be empowered with their own choices.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:58.640
 You know, how will you approach this problem or mistake?

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.640
 And how will you respond?

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:06.600
 And we support them in being their own leader in their world.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:11.040
 And I would like to highlight one program that we did at our alternative high school.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.450
 It's Berkeley Technology Academy.

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:32.360
 And we started this project in the garden last year called RTI in the garden, which was just kind of like, well, how do we provide response to intervention, which is a tool that all, you know, all teachers use when when a kid is struggling with an academic problem or a social emotional problem.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:43.120
 And we were trying to think of, well, how can we respond to an intervention if something's happening in the classroom or something's happening on the campus space and bring them into the garden and support them better.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.120
 So we were really looking at social emotional tools to give to students.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:49.280
 This is a really small high school.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:12:02.110
 A lot of the kids are opting to go there as opposed to Berkeley High School, which is our large campus, because they're not feeling successful there, you know, or maybe they aren't having enough credits to graduate.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:09.080
 And so they come to this school for more support, and they're really small classrooms, and there's a lot of struggle at that school.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:21.690
 So we created different tools through teacher collaboration that will get kids more into the garden, whether they are struggling with, like, outbursts or, you know, trauma that just came in from the streets.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:27.330
 There's, you know, violence that a lot of these kids are experiencing or food insecurity, all these things we wanted to support.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:34.770
 So we gave them some tools of calm and reflection to use in the garden space.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:45.130
 And we've been having great success in just, you know, even if we hit maybe three or four kids and giving them a little bit more reprieve in the garden, we've become successful.

00:12:45.350 --> 00:12:47.570
 And so we do that in different ways.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:50.170
 And I'm really proud of that particular approach right now.

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:58.810
 Kind of following up with that, you've been in the education sector for a couple years now.

00:12:58.930 --> 00:12:59.630
 We have three.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:06.870
 So just over, yeah, so over those three years, how have you seen food education in schools shift?

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:07.690
 Wow.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:14.210
 You know what, I think we're in kind of like, when you're in the work, it feels like everyone's doing it.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:25.200
 And especially in Berkeley, since we have this program at all of our schools and we're expanding and in a really smart way, I feel like everyone's on board.

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:31.060
 But, you know, I remember four years ago, it wasn't the case.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:40.340
 I wouldn't get students reaching out to me or teachers from across the country reaching out to me or other districts reaching out saying, I want to do this.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:46.930
 But now I get districts and principals and teachers reaching out all the time saying, how can we do this as a district model?

00:13:47.370 --> 00:13:57.750
 You know, not as an other or a special entity that comes in and provides, but how can we integrate that into our education, our programming for the district, our funding for the district?

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:01.470
 And it's a great opportunity to kind of demonstrate, well, this is how we've done it here.

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:03.330
 This is how we've rallied the community.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:04.730
 This is how we've budgeted.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:13.420
 All these different details that are not as sexy as whole child development is, is something that we've been able to provide as a support for others.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:16.800
 And I feel like we're in a real movement right now.

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:18.020
 You know, food's everywhere.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:21.260
 We had the last farm bill that was really innovative.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:26.580
 We've got more money for farmers markets and nutrition assistance.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:32.760
 We've got more schools that are getting more money for farm to school grants and even from their general fund.

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:39.940
 So I think it's kind of getting on this more towards a tipping point of universality in conversation.

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:40.600
 What do you think?

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:44.190
 Have you seen a difference in, again, food education?

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:51.370
 Have you seen people that are maybe a little bit more, there's more conversation about food education now than there was four years ago?

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:53.960
 I would definitely agree with that, I think.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:57.280
 But also I've been living in the Bay Area for quite some time now.

00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:04.910
 I grew up in Orange County where food is there, but I think being in the Bay has really changed my perspective of food.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:11.810
 And I know with the sort of boom in the food movement, I think this is a great place for the model to begin.

00:15:12.910 --> 00:15:19.850
 But I think food education is important across so many disciplines, and I've really seen people adapt different lessons.

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:26.970
 And I know we've been talking about it with the rest of the staff, how you can use food for history and math and geography and language arts.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:32.150
 And it's really great to get that exposure to kids, I think.

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:38.730
 I think food is a really powerful tool because it touches on every topic, but also everyone can relate to it.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:43.630
 It's across cultures, it's across disciplines, and it matters to everyone.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:46.990
 The way we grow it, produce it, harvest it, it touches on everything.

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:53.170
 But I also want to take us back three years ago when the program got completely defunded.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:16:01.370
 We had this huge federal grant from the SNAP-Ed bill that was part of the last farm bill.

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:03.770
 A lot of other organizations got this money.

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:10.190
 And when we got rebudgeted, the farm bill got rebudgeted, we all lost this money.

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:14.210
 We got like $1.9 million to do a guardian cooking program.

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:23.980
 Though we were only at schools that were qualified, meaning that we had some schools that were participating in over 50% free and reduced-price meals,

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:28.100
 which is how we identify qualification for schools in terms of income eligibility.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:34.180
 So we weren't at every school, but we had all this money, and we lost all this money about three years ago.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:42.330
 And when I went around to the community and was asking for support and asking for their thoughts or partnerships around what can we do next,

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:44.690
 where are we going with this program, what should we be teaching,

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:51.110
 should we be rethinking any of this, people had no idea that we even existed, which seems kind of odd, right?

00:16:51.190 --> 00:16:55.470
 I mean, Berkeley's known for being a leader in the food movement, but our parents and our teachers are like,

00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:56.950
 what? What's going on?

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:01.050
 And so we had a really great opportunity to start talking about this work.

00:17:01.130 --> 00:17:08.450
 And we have more support now than I think we ever have just because we got really loud, really big, really fast when we had to.

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:10.590
 And we're trying to maintain that momentum.

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:13.170
 And hopefully that has a radial effect beyond our understanding.

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:17.390
 So I really like school gardens, but I've always had this thing that I wonder about them.

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:25.990
 It seems like they're set up to teach students as if they don't already know anything about food or gardening or if there's no,

00:17:26.110 --> 00:17:31.190
 like as if it's coming out of nowhere, which is like a math lesson or something that you learn in school.

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:41.540
 Sometimes educational approaches assume that kids know nothing, which is really not true.

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:42.220
 Right, right.

00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:44.220
 I think Rebecca hit it on the head.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.580
 I mean, that's a problem with education across the board.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:50.660
 But we could definitely talk about that within the garden space.

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:58.730
 How do you access them like the pre-existing knowledge or the wealth of knowledge about food that students and their families have?

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:04.610
 Yeah, well, we were at a professional training yesterday with all of our staff of educators.

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:06.510
 We were talking about just this.

00:18:06.510 --> 00:18:14.090
 And a lot of folks were saying, well, when I open up a garden lesson, I asked, what do you know about flowers?

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:17.830
 And that's an opportunity for the kids to kind of engage.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:23.310
 And then they provide them with an activity that explores flowers or all the different components of flowers, pollination.

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:28.570
 And then we try to re-invite them back into the conversation and say, now what do you want to know?

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:37.080
 So that's something that we're trying to practice across the board in all the gardens so that we are able to meet the kids where they're at.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:38.920
 I mean, that's what all educators want to do.

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:43.020
 We all say we are going to meet the kids where they're at, wherever that may be.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:45.800
 And so we get to fill out each classroom that way.

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:47.540
 And it's important for us to be flexible.

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:56.140
 And I think that, you know, throughout the years of all this drastic change that we've had to go through, our staff of educators have become very flexible, if they weren't already.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:56.940
 And many of them were.

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:59.220
 But we've become really adaptable and really flexible.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:02.690
 And a group that just says, how can we do this?

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:03.850
 Yes, we can.

00:19:03.910 --> 00:19:04.970
 And let's try it.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:10.810
 And so this is something that we're trying to do that's different from, I think, the normative educational practice.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:14.110
 It really embraces, like, different learning styles.

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:16.010
 That's what I found in a cooking classroom.

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:24.610
 Like, you can definitely see the different learning styles that children have in the classroom environment for our cooking classes that we do.

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:33.190
 You know, some kids are really energetic and they want to cut everything and they want to mix everything and, like, they want the job to always be theirs, even though they can't always have it.

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:40.430
 And there are some kids that are just like, I'm okay, just chopping this one thing really slowly for the next 15 minutes.

00:19:40.810 --> 00:19:43.410
 And everybody else can be running around, but, like, I just want to stay here.

00:19:43.790 --> 00:19:52.460
 And the cooking classroom is such a great place to sort of observe that and build upon it and embrace it.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.030
 And that way everybody can just feel like they belong.

00:19:55.390 --> 00:20:00.910
 And that kid who just kind of doesn't want to do much is kind of, hey, will you gather some plates and wash them?

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:02.990
 And they are so happy doing that.

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:07.690
 And I have termed that, like, separating the bakers from the chefs.

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:09.970
 Like, bakers are, like, very measured.

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:11.930
 They like things to be precise.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:16.270
 And the chefs are, like, the kids who are just like, how much salt should I put in?

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:18.210
 And then they pour in, like, half the salt container.

00:20:19.530 --> 00:20:23.070
 But definitely one of the benefits of a cooking classroom.

00:20:23.190 --> 00:20:25.350
 And I'm sure that happens in the gardens as well.

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:35.260
 Yeah, and we have more freedom, and the kids have more freedom in the after school because we're not in that same kind of pressure button that we are during the classroom minutes.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:41.660
 Because if we have to hit on all these different topics as educators, when are we going to have the minutes in the day?

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:45.560
 And then when are we going to be able to test them on whether or not they've captured this information?

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:48.140
 And this is, like, the reality of education.

00:20:48.800 --> 00:20:58.240
 While there's many gaps and problems with that approach, we still need to say, well, how can we support educating the kid on, you know, photosynthesis?

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:04.260
 And, you know, we need to be more mindful of, like, are we guiding them to have conversations and pair share?

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:09.570
 And so we're a little bit more tight, although we want to allow for the freedom within these boundaries.

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:12.190
 It's this interesting dynamic.

00:21:12.690 --> 00:21:15.070
 We have to be, we're a little bit more structured during the garden.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:28.690
 But the after school cooking program is, a nutrition program, is supposed to be a space where the kids can just explore a little bit more, maybe outside of the academic and maybe more in the social emotional.

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:35.270
 Not to say that that's exclusively disconnected from the academic, but that freedom is a real great opportunity.

00:21:35.750 --> 00:21:41.700
 What it makes me think of is a chat I had years ago with Robbie Jaffe, who is one of the founders of Life Lab.

00:21:41.700 --> 00:21:43.580
 And she was saying, you know, it's fascinating.

00:21:43.720 --> 00:21:52.290
 We found at the beginning of the process of funding these school cooking and gardening classes, it was all about science education.

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:57.460
 And it's shifted to nutrition and public health as the justification for it.

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:04.630
 But it sounds like from hearing you talk that there's like, there's something much bigger about food education that, I mean, it's both those things.

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:15.050
 But there's also some way that you are bringing in an experience of being in the world that's important for students.

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:15.990
 Yeah.

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:21.660
 And I feel like that is just, maybe it's me being in my own food bubble, but I feel like that's like preaching to the choir.

00:22:21.660 --> 00:22:40.380
 Like who doesn't, who can't get behind allowing kids an opportunity to like, to like explore, to observe, to use their senses, to have freedom to, you know, be their own caregiver and caretaker within the garden space or, you know, practice in the kitchen classroom.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.910
 You know, that I feel like everyone could get behind that.

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:57.230
 But what's interesting about this work and the history of this work is that it has like a lot of this work that has been considered in other, which is why I'm really cautious about us sounding too boutique and is that it has followed the money.

00:22:57.990 --> 00:23:02.870
 You know, at one point it was the science entities that were saying, oh, we should do this.

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:03.710
 We'll fund you for this.

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:13.750
 And then another point, it was the farm bill that was saying, we should fund you for this because we're having a gap in like, you know, now it's young farming, the young farming community is like dwindling.

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:19.030
 So they're trying to do more like 4-H funding and it was nutrition that was really a problem.

00:23:19.150 --> 00:23:20.830
 And so they're trying to like focus more on nutrition.

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:23.830
 So it does follow that money stream.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:33.270
 And we, we became really heavy in science, not because we've always been heavy in science, but when we lost our funding, we really had to think how are we supporting the classroom minutes.

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:38.170
 And science is a big gap in education, at least in, you know, California standards.

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:47.010
 And so we kind of grabbed onto that, especially since, you know, life standard, life science is so applicable and many other standards are so applicable.

00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:50.170
 We just kind of took that as, you know, an easy cookie.

00:23:50.650 --> 00:24:01.800
 We're also exploring, you know, a lot of other standards and other like whole child development concepts and try not to follow the money, you know, because that's not sustainable.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:04.880
 And we have already learned a really hard lesson about sustainability.

00:24:05.620 --> 00:24:08.720
 So we're trying to be mindful of that lesson moving forward.

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:11.500
 But I think that's kind of goes to like, where's the money coming from?

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:17.380
 And historically, the money has, has been a real guide for this food systems at large, particularly education.

00:24:18.030 --> 00:24:20.650
 Right now is most of the money, the Berkeley soda tax?

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:22.770
 Is that most of what funds this program?

00:24:23.110 --> 00:24:25.030
 That's the majority of our budget right now.

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:27.890
 But we have budgeted so that we're more diverse.

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:33.060
 So we get a good amount of contribution from the district's general fund.

00:24:33.740 --> 00:24:39.860
 And every year I go before the school board and I talk about what we've done in the past and I ask for them to refund us at the same amount.

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:42.340
 Last year we got $350,000.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:44.460
 This year we have $300,000.

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.420
 And hopefully next year we'll have $300,000.

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:53.070
 And moving forward on, it's important that the district puts forth money as well as support for the programming.

00:24:53.270 --> 00:24:59.430
 Often, you know, you can say I support this, but unless there's some kind of pull or dollar amount, it's hard to get that support.

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:05.150
 And they've been funding us at the state, the stage for the last couple of years.

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:13.350
 And then, of course, the soda tax is a wonderful blessing and support that is now a large part of our budget.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:22.390
 We also do other grants from various entities to do special projects like the high school social emotional support was from another grant.

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:28.970
 So we continue to diversify our funding as well as fundraisers to have money to do professional training.

00:25:29.410 --> 00:25:31.290
 So we're trying to be a little bit more diverse.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:36.430
 But, yeah, the soda tax has been a huge part of our budget this year and hopefully increasing next year.

00:25:36.950 --> 00:25:43.790
 So you've also worked to make some fresh produce, particularly farmers markets, more accessible to people without much money.

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:46.710
 Could you talk about your work with the USDA?

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.170
 Well, I've done a lot of work with farmers markets.

00:25:49.490 --> 00:25:51.190
 That's kind of where I started.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:57.460
 And my pull towards farmers markets actually came from urban planning around food systems.

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:00.560
 So that food system planning is my background.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:17.170
 And after graduate school, I wrote this thesis on food security and the role that community food security plays in that and different tools such as farmers markets, community gardens, various things like that.

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:30.110
 And that got picked up by the USDA because I referenced a lot of USDA research and it was in a small moment of time where the USDA was actually looking to bring in young people.

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:48.930
 And so I went straight to D.C. to work on connecting farmers markets with SNAP benefits than food stamps at a time when all the food stamps SNAP, I'll just call SNAP or CalFresh in California.

00:26:48.930 --> 00:26:51.900
 It's called many different things.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:54.300
 So that's when all the benefits were being put on electronic cards.

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.980
 And so farmers markets had been accepting benefits for years.

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:01.580
 And now they were no longer because they didn't have the devices.

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:02.540
 They didn't have electricity.

00:27:02.760 --> 00:27:06.270
 There's all kinds of roadblocks, numerous roadblocks.

00:27:06.410 --> 00:27:10.850
 And then the folks that were going to the farmers markets didn't know how to use their benefits at the farmers markets.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:18.370
 And then when they're finally there, as farmers markets became more popular and in certain neighborhoods, the willingness to pay increased.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:19.630
 So the prices increased.

00:27:19.970 --> 00:27:21.270
 There's all these different issues.

00:27:21.270 --> 00:27:27.360
 And I was in the right moment, the right place to begin to think through a lot of those problems.

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:35.840
 And it was right when the Obama administration was inaugurated and they were bringing on new people to think more deeply about these issues.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:51.700
 And so I've done a lot of work on thinking through, like, connecting those that need the food with those that have the food and creating different projects and programs for the farmers, everyone in between, and the eaters to eat more healthy.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:28:08.180
 So actually, I wrote a handbook on how to use your SNAP benefits at farmers markets at the USDA in, like, 2008, which was, like, such an interesting endeavor because it was – I was kind of like being an investigative reporter.

00:28:08.180 --> 00:28:14.960
 I was going around to all the different agencies within the USDA asking them, you know, how do we do this?

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:16.020
 What's your role?

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:29.740
 And then we kind of compiled them with a lot of different community groups, like the National Farmers' Markets Coalition, which I think is still a real main player in that kind of field of farmers' markets.

00:28:29.940 --> 00:28:30.920
 And they played a role.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:32.970
 And it was really very collaborative.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:38.600
 SNAP benefits are just – you can have a whole season about just SNAP.

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:39.320
 It's insane.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:40.440
 SNAP and WIC.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:41.200
 You have WIC benefits.

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:49.280
 You have WIC benefits, and then now you have Market Match, which is California's farmers' markets matching program.

00:28:50.200 --> 00:29:02.480
 And so I was working at a nonprofit called Roots of Change when that was becoming more of a support to getting more farmers' market participants to come to the market and use their benefits.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:06.400
 And it's when you could use your SNAP benefits or WIC benefits.

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:22.780
 And so when I was at this nonprofit, we were working with a lot of farmers' market organizations and hunger organizations throughout California from San Diego all the way up to Humboldt to help think through ways of getting more people to use their benefits and where the gaps needed.

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:34.220
 And we got a large grant from the USDA, and we worked with Wholesome Way, which is a national organization, to start taking what we've learned in California to change national policy.

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:39.440
 And that has since grown to now be a federal rule, I believe.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:55.260
 Now there's benefits that are funding that can be applied for through grants to fund programs like Market Match, which provide more money to buy more fruits and vegetables exclusively to those that are accessing SNAP and WIC.

00:29:56.310 --> 00:29:59.650
 So that's kind of – that's a huge, like, revolution.

00:29:59.650 --> 00:30:19.960
 So it seems like you've done a lot of work on accessibility, whether it be for fresh produce or now for children in education, kind of wondering about where you see yourself in the food movement, kind of where you kind of began your journey and how it has transformed.

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:24.880
 Well, I'm hoping that it's more of a cycle or a circle.

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:32.170
 Another topic that we talk a lot about in the garden, in the kitchen, is life cycles, food cycles.

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:38.550
 I think that my career has been a bit of a cycle.

00:30:38.810 --> 00:30:49.930
 It seems like I've approached it, you know, with the intention of coming from the built environment and then turning it, you know, turning more towards, you know, the social environment.

00:30:50.940 --> 00:31:04.810
 So I, you know, I go where I'm needed, you know, I think that if you work in food systems and you think about systems at large and you're a systems-oriented person,

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:24.200
 I mean, that you understand all the different factors that come into play to help ideate different solutions to complex problems, which is what I, you know, strive to do, then I will be going where the solutions are needed.

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:27.080
 And I hope to continue to do that.

00:31:27.180 --> 00:31:34.560
 I feel really fortunate that I've been able to work with a lot of folks that are in this kind of small world of food systems right now, but it's growing.

00:31:35.340 --> 00:31:40.600
 And so I'm happy to continue to work on different projects as they pull me in and out and as we create our own here.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.200
 So I have no idea what's next.

00:31:45.440 --> 00:31:52.170
 What do you draw on from the broader food movement for your work in school gardening and cooking program?

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:59.820
 Well, I think I've been in this field for maybe nine years, which isn't a long time.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:05.040
 There's a lot of people that have been working in this field for 30 years.

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:17.400
 So throughout these nine years, I've been able to work with the same people over and over again, whether I'm in D.C. or I'm in Virginia or I'm in Portland or California or wherever.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:27.020
 There's been a group of folks working in food systems at large from the beginning of my career that I've been able to kind of pull in and out of.

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:34.490
 And like I mentioned before, you know, food systems is really broad and it touches on science and labor and all these different things.

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:40.360
 So I have an opportunity to bring in people that I've worked well with and people that I want to work with.

00:32:40.420 --> 00:32:44.920
 I've always been, you know, thinking about working with because they're leading all these different efforts.

00:32:45.020 --> 00:32:55.640
 I've been able to bring them in to help us think about, well, how should we teach language in the garden or how should we teach about soda consumption in the kitchen classroom?

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:58.840
 You know, that's not really a published curriculum right now.

00:32:58.880 --> 00:33:00.580
 There's a lot of different lessons.

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:02.540
 There's rethink your drink, which is great.

00:33:02.540 --> 00:33:03.920
 There's a lot of after-school programming.

00:33:04.380 --> 00:33:10.820
 We've been supported by the Nutrition Policy Institute over at the University of California.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:18.700
 And I'm able to continue to work with these folks to rethink all these different topics that we had before us.

00:33:18.940 --> 00:33:22.230
 So I am able to bring them in so many different ways.

00:33:23.170 --> 00:33:42.580
 My good friend, Sarah Nelson, who's also Rebecca's other boss, not to make this like it's nepotistic or anything, but I constantly reach out to her for advice around, you know, whether it's what kind of tools you use in the kitchen classroom that you see as a best tool for second graders.

00:33:43.330 --> 00:33:51.370
 Or, you know, she helped us with that training so that around engaging kids in the kitchen classroom with some of our new new staff.

00:33:51.530 --> 00:33:52.810
 And that was really great.

00:33:52.810 --> 00:34:00.830
 And we were able to work with Whitney Cohen from Life Lab over at UC Santa Cruz to help us think through science standards in the garden.

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:08.900
 And like I said before, the Nutrition Policy Institute, Pat Crawford, who's a leader in nutrition, education, policy, research.

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:15.170
 She's just such a force in this field of, you know, nutrition and food systems at large.

00:34:15.330 --> 00:34:20.590
 We've been able to work with her on creating a curriculum that we're trying out in our kitchen classrooms.

00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:24.070
 You want to talk about the curriculum for a little bit?

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:26.510
 Because I know that's a lot of what you're up to right now.

00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:29.070
 Yeah, always developing new curriculum.

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:39.650
 Well, part of our strategy for communicating what we do and advocating for what we do is to show what we do.

00:34:40.160 --> 00:34:43.960
 And we've developed a curriculum that does just that.

00:34:44.690 --> 00:34:54.120
 The curriculum is also a great tool that we could use that we're doing not the same thing, but that we're touching on the same topics across schools.

00:34:54.200 --> 00:35:04.800
 So that we don't have one school over here that's just like totally killing it with garden education because they've got a real lead garden educator that's just been doing this for a long time.

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:07.560
 And they are using their own curriculum and they're pulling all these different things.

00:35:07.820 --> 00:35:12.080
 We want to make sure that all of our schools are killing it like that, you know, in that really good way.

00:35:12.640 --> 00:35:15.800
 And so the curriculum allows us to do that.

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:27.440
 And we've developed the curriculum collaboratively with curriculum developers and, you know, garden education, with input from garden staff that we have on our team, with standard specialists.

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:34.060
 And we brought in our science teachers and we glean from the broad world of garden curriculum, which is massive.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:41.050
 And we've been able to pick and pull which standards we want to try and which lessons align best.

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:45.050
 And we tried them out the first year in all of our school gardens.

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:50.030
 And we had an assessment to measure what we were doing and how we could incorporate the change.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:56.110
 And then we published our first edition of garden-based learning for primary school at the beginning of this year.

00:35:56.390 --> 00:36:01.910
 And we're going to be publishing our second edition at the end of this year because we're making a lot of changes because we're always flexible.

00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:03.110
 We're always learning from each other.

00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:09.400
 And we're always improving, especially now that we have a base curriculum that we can say, well, this is the base level of measurement.

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:13.560
 It's easy to change something when you have a starting point.

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:17.680
 And so we're trying to do the same thing in our after-school kitchen classrooms.

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:20.760
 We're reaching out to community partners.

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:23.900
 We're gleaning from the broad world of nutrition and cooking education.

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:32.280
 And we're trying to pick and choose different lessons that we think would hit our goals, which is not just around sugar consumption and sugar awareness.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:33.520
 Those are very strong ones.

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:39.500
 But also engaging in student behavior change and whole child development.

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:46.540
 And so we're trying to kind of backwards design that one, meaning that we've got these lessons.

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:47.480
 We're trying them out.

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:48.460
 We're getting back together.

00:36:48.580 --> 00:36:49.920
 We're saying what's working, what's not working.

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:53.360
 And then we'll try it again next year with assessments.

00:36:53.360 --> 00:36:57.180
 Now that after we have our base curriculum and then we'll try it again the next year.

00:36:57.180 --> 00:37:05.920
 And hopefully we'll continue that cycle and we'll continue to develop curriculum at all the different schools that, you know, we need to.

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:09.800
 We've begun developing a curriculum for RTI in the garden last year.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:18.460
 So we're still in that phase of getting all the different lessons and continuing our teacher collaboration so that we can have a base for assessments.

00:37:19.080 --> 00:37:21.340
 Well, Jezra spoke mostly to the gardening curriculum.

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:28.140
 That's something that's been, as she said, developed over a couple of years with our after school cooking curriculum.

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:32.240
 We have we are doing like the first round of assessments now.

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:39.070
 It's a very like we're kind of jumping in, seeing what's working, adapting as necessary, making notes.

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:53.800
 We were presented with a giant three ring binder, like the three inch size of activities and recipes and kind of, you know, I had the prompt of pick some and see how they work.

00:37:54.020 --> 00:37:57.720
 So we're we're also kind of testing them as they go.

00:37:57.860 --> 00:38:01.360
 And it's been it's been really great to see like what works, what doesn't work.

00:38:01.480 --> 00:38:13.780
 Some of the activities are obviously a little older and you have to keep in mind that classrooms change and like kids are changing as you have like different generations growing up with different technologies and they learn in different ways.

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:31.690
 So sometimes it's it's a something as simple as instead of having like a printed out label, like having a physical like object, like the actual soda bottle instead of like a picture of the nutrition label on the soda bottle for a more experiential learning.

00:38:31.690 --> 00:38:39.190
 I suppose when you also get all the kids that just want to drink the soda from the bottle, if you haven't emptied it all the time.

00:38:40.800 --> 00:38:41.810
 Yeah, for now.

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:42.130
 Yeah.

00:38:42.290 --> 00:38:49.320
 But we're hoping that with some, you know, trial and error, we're figuring it out slowly.

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:51.300
 And like I've been meeting with Tim a lot.

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:58.700
 Tim's been doing a lot of the work now on sort of piecing together this curriculum puzzle.

00:39:00.980 --> 00:39:07.500
 Tim's our health and wellness coordinator who's leading these projects with folks like Rebecca's incredible support.

00:39:08.380 --> 00:39:10.620
 I don't know if you're dumb, but it's the curriculum.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:21.940
 It's applicability and a success is reliant upon people like Rebecca trying them out, saying yes, and having the astuteness to figure out how to scale it up and scale it down.

00:39:22.040 --> 00:39:24.960
 So that's appropriate to the students that they're working with.

00:39:25.020 --> 00:39:28.020
 We work with, you know, K through high school.

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:36.020
 So it's really champions like Rebecca that, you know, figures this out with our, you know, collaborative team when we get back together.

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:40.360
 But it takes a very certain type of leader to do this.

00:39:41.640 --> 00:39:48.340
 It's like you're developing a curriculum about hands-on learning by doing hands-on learning, right?

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:48.720
 All right.

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:50.700
 Well, here's the thing that we want you to do.

00:39:50.780 --> 00:39:53.200
 You just have to do it to figure out how to do it.

00:39:53.520 --> 00:40:00.750
 But it's really fun, you know, like figure out how first graders, like first graders are just a really fun group in general.

00:40:00.970 --> 00:40:14.430
 I really like working with first graders where you take something like we were going to do an activity like we wanted to the kids to take a picture of a plant and like separate it by what part of the planet was that you eat.

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:22.020
 And then we got to the class and the kids were all over the place and they could barely remember the six parts of the plant.

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:25.320
 They could remember like three and we were like, all right, this has to be adjusted.

00:40:25.800 --> 00:40:26.620
 Fruits and vegetables.

00:40:26.900 --> 00:40:27.120
 All right.

00:40:27.160 --> 00:40:29.480
 Let's separate these into fruits and vegetables.

00:40:29.740 --> 00:40:36.450
 And just something as simple as like, you know, thinking on your feet and being like, how can I still get these kids to learn something with?

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:41.990
 We have one hour to teach them a lesson and prepare a recipe.

00:40:42.350 --> 00:40:45.370
 So that requires a lot of fine tuning.

00:40:46.530 --> 00:40:49.890
 It's not like an hour and a half is like the ideal time for that.

00:40:49.990 --> 00:40:56.070
 So to like kind of cramp it down into one hour and figure out like, what can a first grader cut?

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:58.510
 What will a first grader do?

00:40:58.810 --> 00:41:05.990
 First graders don't necessarily care that they can't flip the pancake, but fifth graders really care that they can't flip a pancake and make their own.

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:12.100
 So there's a lot of adjustments, but that's why I really love this job.

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:18.410
 That's why I love this program because you're doing, you know, every, I guess we haven't talked about how many classes these kids get.

00:41:18.410 --> 00:41:25.330
 So in the afterschool program, the kids will be receiving four weeks of the program, which is two classes a week.

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:27.610
 So total of eight one hour lessons.

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:29.830
 And then we get a whole new batch of kids.

00:41:30.090 --> 00:41:36.030
 So it's really fun to sort of adapt as each different group of kids comes.

00:41:36.250 --> 00:41:50.790
 But I think it makes creating a curriculum really difficult because, you know, I, I'm sure every educator out there has received a lesson, been told that like this lesson for first graders is going to take 10 minutes and gone.

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:51.690
 Yeah.

00:41:51.690 --> 00:41:51.790
 Yeah.

00:41:51.730 --> 00:41:51.970
 Right.

00:41:51.970 --> 00:41:58.230
 There's no way that first graders can do this in 10 minutes and it's being aware of that.

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:08.520
 And also just knowing how to change that lesson for your particular group, which has been really, you know, keep me on my toes, really exciting.

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:09.690
 I enjoy it.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:16.670
 So this is how you, clearly this is how you develop the curriculum for, for the rest of the school district, right?

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:17.590
 By trial.

00:42:17.810 --> 00:42:21.950
 Is this, um, is this also how you take over the world?

00:42:22.740 --> 00:42:24.140
 Through radical education?

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:24.800
 Maybe.

00:42:25.380 --> 00:42:30.030
 Is this how, do you, does this become a curriculum people use beyond Berkeley?

00:42:30.470 --> 00:42:30.770
 Yeah.

00:42:30.810 --> 00:42:31.390
 And that's the point.

00:42:31.390 --> 00:42:37.010
 That's why we, we publish it and why we go around to different, why I go around to different conferences and talk about what we're doing.

00:42:37.350 --> 00:42:41.920
 Um, because like Rebecca just explained, you know, we have different boundaries.

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:45.040
 We could only teach for an hour because of the afterschool constraints.

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:46.540
 We may not have X, Y, Z.

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:48.980
 We have a couple of lessons that we have to touch on.

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:49.820
 How do we do that?

00:42:50.220 --> 00:42:51.120
 This is reality.

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:54.500
 You know, this is what other school districts and other educators are up against.

00:42:54.500 --> 00:43:02.360
 And if we're doing the hard work of figuring out at least some examples of how to do it in within reality, then that's a great tool.

00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:07.560
 Um, and it's a great tool for them to then take that and say, this is a curriculum that we want to try.

00:43:07.660 --> 00:43:15.500
 It's already been kind of vetted with some data to get, you know, funding or school board supports or even a school principal support.

00:43:16.000 --> 00:43:19.730
 So if this is how we take over the world, then sure.

00:43:19.730 --> 00:43:35.430
 But I also want to reference, you know, the cycle of the curriculum and the process of inviting our educators like Rebecca to be involved in the process as a teacher and as a student.

00:43:35.650 --> 00:43:49.250
 I think that's kind of the radical teacher in our team is that they're open to learning all the time and they're open to trying, you know, these these ideas and lessons and open to learning from students and each other.

00:43:49.250 --> 00:43:54.740
 And then also leading and saying, you know, making amendments to what they're doing.

00:43:55.140 --> 00:43:58.050
 I think that's kind of that's a great opportunity that we have.

00:43:58.190 --> 00:43:58.510
 Yeah.

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:09.960
 And I get a hold all of this in a big, not too tight hug and to make sure that we're hitting all of our targets and getting all the different funders invested.

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:20.220
 And we have many different stakeholders and though, you know, what what happens on the ground is wonderful and that's the important work that we have.

00:44:20.300 --> 00:44:23.900
 And I get a hold it all and to make sure that we're hitting all of our targets.

00:44:24.100 --> 00:44:27.820
 I feel like in education, you kind of you always want it your way.

00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:32.120
 I feel like probably most teachers think that, like, I just want to do everything my way.

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:36.300
 But you can't you obviously can't always have it your way because there are limitations.

00:44:37.240 --> 00:44:46.710
 But it's I think it's a lot about knowing when you need to make the case that, like, it needs to be your way, because that's maybe something that works across the board.

00:44:46.790 --> 00:44:49.850
 And it's definitely how curriculum gets developed.

00:44:50.050 --> 00:44:54.670
 Like, we found this one thing works really well for all ages.

00:44:54.790 --> 00:45:01.970
 So this is if this is one thing they told us we can't do, but we found that it works really well, then I'd rather, like, make sure that that thing happens.

00:45:01.970 --> 00:45:06.410
 The next conversation would be, well, you know, why is this really important?

00:45:07.170 --> 00:45:08.590
 You know, where's the value in this?

00:45:09.370 --> 00:45:15.770
 And how can we talk about it in a way that invites the maybe the folks that say, no, we can't do this.

00:45:15.950 --> 00:45:27.390
 So there's always a approach to a problem by inviting other people in because we turn it into, well, you know, we need to talk about sugar, but we also need to talk about nutrition.

00:45:27.790 --> 00:45:32.890
 And so we guide the conversations towards how do we hit all of our targets.

00:45:33.610 --> 00:45:35.530
 It's a lot of it's a lot of people pleasing.

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:38.800
 I mean, yeah, that's like life.

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:44.780
 I would say, you know, we have a community here and we all need to work together.

00:45:45.340 --> 00:45:49.820
 And I might I might think that one way is the right way, but I could be very wrong.

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:50.560
 Right.

00:45:50.600 --> 00:46:03.280
 So as long as I can communicate why I think this is right and be open to others opinions and experiences, then there's there's a great movement towards education in the way that we want to affect our kids.

00:46:03.480 --> 00:46:18.430
 And I think we I think we do that, you know, I'd say that that is definitely the most important part, the whole you want to do it, but also super open to hearing other people's opinions and not being really stubborn about your own.

00:46:18.430 --> 00:46:22.510
 I think, well, learning is such a great gift, you know, like learning is a great gift.

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:30.090
 Being able to teach is a great gift and acknowledging that those have to happen on an equilibrium is very important.

00:46:30.970 --> 00:46:31.830
 Amen.

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:35.410
 Why does it matter?

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:40.150
 I guess that's the I guess that's you mentioned that as we're working it out with other people.

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:44.170
 Like, what what is the big picture of why it's important to do this?

00:46:44.330 --> 00:46:52.820
 This kind of food education, you know, there could be a naysayer from every angle and a response to every naysayer.

00:46:53.000 --> 00:47:05.280
 I've had folks come at us with, you know, we don't have money for this because it is not it's not part of like the core educational goals.

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:12.370
 And there's an argument with no, you know, there's here's how we approach the core educational goals.

00:47:12.530 --> 00:47:14.070
 You know, we've just talked about that with a curriculum.

00:47:14.890 --> 00:47:24.080
 And I've had folks say, you know, we, you know, we have no money for like, where do I begin?

00:47:24.220 --> 00:47:24.840
 Where do I begin?

00:47:25.840 --> 00:47:30.140
 People don't have enough money to purchase healthy food at the grocery store.

00:47:30.300 --> 00:47:35.790
 Why should we give them opportunities to cook healthy food if they can't even purchase the healthy food?

00:47:35.790 --> 00:47:51.390
 So then we come at it with like, we're providing another educational or an experience opportunity to think about purchasing on a limited budget with for healthy food, whether that's, you know, not just exclusively growing your own food.

00:47:51.390 --> 00:48:04.860
 But we educate around budgeting and finances, and we firmly believe that, you know, we could purchase healthy food on a budget where we have to teach people how to cook in bulk and how to buy in season and all these different things.

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:08.960
 And so there's an argument for every naysayer around why this education is important.

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:13.260
 And these are tools that people take away throughout their life.

00:48:13.960 --> 00:48:17.960
 I know I had some aha moments with food when I was very young.

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:24.340
 And, you know, they might seem insignificant in retelling them, but they've stuck with me for a long time.

00:48:24.480 --> 00:48:42.560
 So I'm hopeful that the kids that are in our district, in our gardens and kitchens, have these moments that may not be tangible, but that will affect them, you know, when they have their own families or when they're up against having, you know, a small budget, but needing to take care of their own health.

00:48:42.960 --> 00:48:53.220
 Or wanting to support the larger ecosystem and believing in sustainable solutions for our environment and our labor and that everyone matters.

00:48:53.380 --> 00:48:57.300
 I mean, there's so many different reasons why food systems is an important education.

00:48:57.860 --> 00:49:04.080
 I think, I mean, I can speak mostly from maybe in the classroom because that's where I've been a lot.

00:49:04.080 --> 00:49:12.460
 And I've just seen a lot of opinions change with children and over such a short time as like four to six weeks.

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:19.010
 I think food education just provides kids with a lot, kind of like a space to grow.

00:49:19.330 --> 00:49:26.800
 And I think on the broader scheme of education, having food education has sort of created a more dynamic classroom.

00:49:27.040 --> 00:49:28.540
 I think that's really important.

00:49:28.540 --> 00:49:39.490
 I think as a whole, we're sort of moving away from a stagnant classroom of like sitting and just taking notes and just taking tests and like lots of numbers based.

00:49:40.150 --> 00:49:45.670
 And I've just seen food create, as I said, this dynamic classroom where everybody gets to move.

00:49:45.670 --> 00:49:52.750
 And there's a lot of different moving parts, such as a restaurant kitchen to sort of keep that metaphor within food.

00:49:52.750 --> 00:49:58.410
 And I just think that that's been really important as a starting point for kids' creativity.

00:49:58.790 --> 00:50:07.140
 I've seen a lot of kids also start brainstorming their own recipes in the classroom, which I think is amazing.

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:12.480
 Food is such a creative discipline, I guess.

00:50:12.540 --> 00:50:21.910
 It's not necessarily a discipline, but to have a child see like look in the garden and be like, it would be great to eat these like tomatoes with basil.

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:24.870
 And maybe they didn't even know that that was a thing before.

00:50:24.870 --> 00:50:29.190
 And to see them sort of put those things together is amazing.

00:50:29.190 --> 00:50:42.450
 Also, as someone who studied anthropology in college, it also like really sort of fires off that sense of what I've learned and how food is so important as our culture.

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:48.210
 And I think for kids to learn about their own culture through food is also really important.

00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:53.070
 And it just I could go on forever about all the different things that food that food hits.

00:50:53.150 --> 00:50:54.050
 And I'm sure I have.

00:50:54.050 --> 00:50:57.510
 My friends are definitely tired of me talking about it.

00:50:57.510 --> 00:51:10.750
 But I think just as a whole, the most important thing that I've seen is that dynamic classroom environment and just seeing kids want to listen to you because they know that you have something really important to say.

00:51:10.910 --> 00:51:23.370
 But then that you are also going to let them go, go a little crazy and stick their hands in things and, you know, be hands on and be energetic and not be told no.

00:51:23.450 --> 00:51:30.920
 Like I said earlier, I think that, you know, we've covered it.

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:31.860
 Yeah, I think that's a good one.

00:51:31.860 --> 00:51:32.260
 Yeah.

00:51:32.920 --> 00:51:34.880
 I don't think I could add anything else.

00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:36.600
 Yeah.

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:38.900
 Well, thanks so much.

00:51:38.960 --> 00:51:39.640
 I appreciate it.

00:51:39.680 --> 00:51:41.200
 This is it's been a great conversation.

00:51:41.340 --> 00:51:43.520
 And it was awesome to talk to you to both of you together.

00:51:43.760 --> 00:51:44.800
 Yeah, likewise.

00:51:45.120 --> 00:51:51.160
 Thank you for the opportunity for this conversation in this environment for like such an open space to talk about food systems.

00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:54.040
 And for Rebecca and I to be able to talk like this has been really special.

00:51:54.760 --> 00:51:56.830
 Thanks for hiring me.

